2026-11-24T16:08:07 | Claude chunk C | found | Research, Win+Alt+\\ in WebView2 (short):\n- Windows hands any Win combination that is not a registered shell hotkey to the focused window as a normal keystroke (with Alt held it is WM_SYSKEYDOWN): https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/inputdev/about-keyboard-input . I found no documentation that Win+Alt+\\ (VK_OEM_5) is a shell hotkey; the known shell ones are Win+Alt+R/G/B/D/PrtScn/Enter (Game Bar, clock) etc. RegisterHotKey docs say Win-key shortcuts are reserved for the OS and a registration of a taken combination fails: https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/api/winuser/nf-winuser-registerhotkey\n- WebView2 raises the host's AcceleratorKeyPressed when Ctrl or Alt is held (so Win+Alt+\\ qualifies), before the page: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.web.webview2.core.corewebview2controller.acceleratorkeypressed . Historic gap: Alt combinations not forwarded to a host window unless AllowHostInputProcessing is used (opposite direction, host to page is fine): https://weblog.west-wind.com/posts/2025/Aug/20/Using-the-new-WebView2-AllowHostInputProcessing-Keyboard-Mapping-Feature ; issue thread https://github.com/MicrosoftEdge/WebView2Feedback/issues/1703\n- Conclusion: expect e.metaKey && e.altKey && e.code === "Backslash" to reach the page keydown (use e.code, not e.key, because Win+Alt can change the produced character and the layout differs). NOT verified with real OS keystrokes: the hidden desktop gets no real keyboard input and CDP key events bypass the shell, so this is documentation-based. If the owner finds it swallowed: first choice is the page handler on e.code; fallback is RegisterHotKey(MOD_WIN|MOD_ALT, VK_OEM_5) on the main window handle (fails if the shell owns it), or a low-level keyboard hook. Not built.
